WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.205
Federal HR 2.0, the program OPM runs,

2
00:00:03.205 --> 00:00:08.500
moves agencies' core HR records
onto one Oracle platform.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.051
If an agency builds HR-driven
identity on top of it,

4
00:00:12.051 --> 00:00:17.335
so that a hire in HR becomes an account
and a departure removes one, the data

5
00:00:17.335 --> 00:00:20.280
crosses Oracle's cloud, Microsoft's cloud,

6
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.225
the agency's own network,
and possibly AWS.

7
00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:26.950
Every one of those hops
starts with a DNS lookup.

8
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:32.163
DNS, the domain name system, turns
a name into a network address.

9
00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:34.750
This explainer answers three questions.

10
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.562
Which names must resolve where?

11
00:00:36.562 --> 00:00:37.574
What breaks?

12
00:00:37.574 --> 00:00:43.038
And when do you need private DNS or
forwarding, instead of ordinary public DNS?

13
00:00:43.038 --> 00:00:47.163
It's built for architects still
learning, so terms are defined as we go.

14
00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:48.963
This is research under review.

15
00:00:48.963 --> 00:00:53.447
Every fact comes from sources
opened on October twenty-second,

16
00:00:53.447 --> 00:00:55.949
2026, mostly vendor documentation.

17
00:00:55.949 --> 00:00:59.950
A few Microsoft facts come from
search excerpts of Microsoft Learn

18
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:03.450
pages rather than full pages, and
I'll say so when they come up.

19
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:08.250
Where I drew a conclusion that no source
states directly, I'll call it an inference.

20
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.638
And recommendations are labeled
program design guidance.

21
00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:18.132
Sources: Research brief, DNS for
HR-driven identity across Oracle,

22
00:01:18.132 --> 00:01:23.182
Microsoft, on-premises,
VMware and AWS (verified Oct.

23
00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:30.036
22, 2026), Method and Bottom line;
the Federal Modernization Workbook's

24
00:01:30.036 --> 00:01:36.040
Book 03 Ch03 (OPM as the Federal
HR 2.0 program owner); Book 08.

25
00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:40.585
Here's the chain end to end,
as Microsoft's Oracle tutorial

26
00:01:40.585 --> 00:01:44.702
and the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 03 describe it.

27
00:01:44.702 --> 00:01:51.015
It starts in Oracle Fusion Cloud HCM,
the HR system Oracle runs as a service.

28
00:01:51.015 --> 00:01:53.778
HCM means human capital management.

29
00:01:53.778 --> 00:01:56.970
Changes leave Fusion as
extracts, or as Atom feeds,

30
00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.940
which are lists of recent changes
that another program polls.

31
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.927
In this pattern, the
middleware pulls from Oracle.

32
00:02:03.927 --> 00:02:06.290
The middleware is the
program in the middle.

33
00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:11.185
Microsoft says it can run in Oracle
Integration Cloud, called OIC,

34
00:02:11.185 --> 00:02:16.352
or in Azure as an Azure Function, a
Logic App, or a Data Factory pipeline.

35
00:02:16.352 --> 00:02:21.290
It turns HR records into a standard
format called SCIM and posts them to

36
00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:26.227
Microsoft Graph, Microsoft's main API,
at an endpoint called bulkUpload.

37
00:02:26.227 --> 00:02:29.515
That's Entra's API-driven
inbound provisioning.

38
00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:33.677
The Entra provisioning service then
writes the user into Entra ID.

39
00:02:33.677 --> 00:02:36.472
For users who still live
in Active Directory,

40
00:02:36.472 --> 00:02:40.474
it hands the change to a provisioning
agent: a small service on an

41
00:02:40.474 --> 00:02:45.232
on-premises server that makes outbound
connections only, and writes into AD.

42
00:02:45.232 --> 00:02:50.291
Lifecycle Workflows then run joiner and
leaver tasks on the hire and leave dates.

43
00:02:50.291 --> 00:02:52.340
Notice the DNS pattern.

44
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.952
Every arrow in the top row
crosses a vendor's public name.

45
00:02:55.952 --> 00:02:59.841
Only the agent-to-AD
hop needs internal DNS.

46
00:02:59.841 --> 00:03:07.146
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Configure Oracle
HCM for automatic user provisioning [S1],

47
00:03:07.146 --> 00:03:14.134
Perform bulkUpload [S5], On-premises
application provisioning architecture [S6

48
00:03:14.134 --> 00:03:20.593
(excerpt)], Plan cloud HR application to
Entra user provisioning [S8 (excerpt)],

49
00:03:20.593 --> 00:03:25.570
Lifecycle workflows attribute
sync [S11 (excerpt)]; Oracle,

50
00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:31.020
Working with Atom [S18]; Book
03 Ch05, Provisioning Services.

51
00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.132
That leads to the key idea of this talk.

52
00:03:34.132 --> 00:03:38.120
Almost every name in this path
is public and owned by a vendor.

53
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.974
The Fusion host, which looks
like your environment's name,

54
00:03:41.974 --> 00:03:44.843
then dot f-a dot o-c-s
dot oraclecloud dot com.

55
00:03:44.843 --> 00:03:47.795
Microsoft Graph and the
Entra sign-in hosts.

56
00:03:47.795 --> 00:03:50.183
The provisioning agent's relay names.

57
00:03:50.183 --> 00:03:54.520
And the Amazon Connect names that
contact-center agents' browsers use.

58
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.376
The agency's job is to
resolve those correctly

59
00:03:57.376 --> 00:04:00.007
from every resolver a component can reach.

60
00:04:00.007 --> 00:04:03.558
A resolver is the DNS
server a machine asks.

61
00:04:03.558 --> 00:04:08.820
The agency must never host or shadow
those names in a private zone of its own.

62
00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.808
We'll see why on the failure slide.

63
00:04:10.808 --> 00:04:15.468
Private DNS is needed, along with
forwarding, which means one resolver

64
00:04:15.468 --> 00:04:19.969
passing a query to another that knows
the answer, in only four places.

65
00:04:19.969 --> 00:04:25.124
One: names the agency owns, like AD
domains and their service records,

66
00:04:25.124 --> 00:04:31.107
the middleware's evidence store, internal
HR or identity APIs, and VMware hosts.

67
00:04:31.107 --> 00:04:36.101
Two: cloud private endpoints the middleware
uses, such as a storage account behind

68
00:04:36.101 --> 00:04:41.020
Azure Private Link, which gives a cloud
service a private address in your network.

69
00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:45.220
Three: cloud private zones that
on-premises systems must read.

70
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.483
Four: AWS interface
endpoints with private DNS.

71
00:04:49.483 --> 00:04:52.521
Everything else is ordinary
public resolution.

72
00:04:52.521 --> 00:05:02.627
Sources: Research brief, Bottom line items
1 and 2, citing Microsoft Learn [S2, S4,

73
00:05:02.627 --> 00:05:10.207
S10], Oracle [S20, S23], AWS [S27–S29,
S31], Broadcom [S36]; the Federal

74
00:05:10.207 --> 00:05:16.365
Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 Ch07 and Ch08;

75
00:05:16.365 --> 00:05:21.320
research_multicloud.md [prior-M S57].

76
00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:26.083
This table condenses the brief's
what-must-resolve-where table.

77
00:05:26.083 --> 00:05:28.169
The full version is in the backup.

78
00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:33.465
The HR middleware, if it runs as Azure
Functions or Logic Apps Standard joined to a

79
00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:36.231
virtual network, resolves the Fusion host,

80
00:05:36.231 --> 00:05:39.945
Graph and sign-in publicly,
through the agency's resolver.

81
00:05:39.945 --> 00:05:43.905
Its evidence store and key vault
sit behind private endpoints,

82
00:05:43.905 --> 00:05:48.245
so it needs a linked private zone, or
a resolver that forwards to Azure.

83
00:05:48.245 --> 00:05:52.525
Microsoft documents an app
setting, WEBSITE_DNS_SERVER,

84
00:05:52.525 --> 00:05:55.782
for pointing the app at
your own DNS server.

85
00:05:55.782 --> 00:06:01.270
Lifecycle Workflow extensions run in
Consumption Logic Apps, the multitenant kind.

86
00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:04.545
As an inference, they can
reach only public names.

87
00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:09.551
The provisioning agent resolves domain
controller locator records internally,

88
00:06:09.551 --> 00:06:12.495
and Microsoft's relay and
sign-in names publicly.

89
00:06:12.495 --> 00:06:17.017
On-premises systems reach cloud private
names by forwarding from the Grid,

90
00:06:17.017 --> 00:06:21.029
meaning the Infoblox appliances,
to each cloud's inbound endpoint:

91
00:06:21.029 --> 00:06:25.333
an address inside the cloud network
that accepts queries from outside.

92
00:06:25.333 --> 00:06:31.082
Fusion users on Oracle's private access
still get Fusion's public DNS answer.

93
00:06:31.082 --> 00:06:34.646
A Lambda function in a VPC,
a virtual private cloud,

94
00:06:34.646 --> 00:06:38.732
reaches agency APIs through
an outbound rule to the Grid.

95
00:06:38.732 --> 00:06:44.746
And vCenter and ESXi need forward and
reverse records, A and PTR, in the Grid.

96
00:06:44.746 --> 00:06:54.956
Sources: Research brief §8, What must
resolve where, and the sources in each row:

97
00:06:54.956 --> 00:07:02.736
Microsoft Learn [S2, S4, S4b (excerpt),
S8 (excerpt), S11b (excerpt),

98
00:07:02.736 --> 00:07:10.192
S13–S15 (excerpts)]; Oracle [S20–S24];
AWS [S29, S30]; Broadcom [S36];

99
00:07:10.192 --> 00:07:18.133
the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08 Ch07 and Ch08;

100
00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:23.690
research_multicloud.md [prior-M S31, S57].

101
00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.384
Next, the government clouds
use different names,

102
00:07:27.384 --> 00:07:29.665
and getting this wrong fails quietly.

103
00:07:29.665 --> 00:07:32.740
Microsoft Graph has a
separate address per cloud.

104
00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:38.474
A GCC tenant, Microsoft's government
community cloud, keeps using the worldwide

105
00:07:38.474 --> 00:07:44.034
endpoints: graph dot microsoft dot com,
and login dot microsoftonline dot com.

106
00:07:44.034 --> 00:07:47.509
A GCC High tenant uses
graph dot microsoft dot us,

107
00:07:47.509 --> 00:07:50.202
and login dot microsoftonline dot us.

108
00:07:50.202 --> 00:07:54.939
The higher government cloud has its own
Graph host and the same dot-us sign-in.

109
00:07:54.939 --> 00:07:57.869
Microsoft says access
tokens from one national

110
00:07:57.869 --> 00:08:00.578
cloud aren't interchangeable
with another's.

111
00:08:00.578 --> 00:08:02.903
The provisioning agent has its own list.

112
00:08:02.903 --> 00:08:06.206
In the public cloud, which
GCC tenants also use,

113
00:08:06.206 --> 00:08:12.203
it reaches relay names under msappproxy
dot net and servicebus dot windows dot net.

114
00:08:12.203 --> 00:08:12.743
In the U.S.

115
00:08:12.743 --> 00:08:16.915
Government cloud those become
msappproxy dot us and servicebus

116
00:08:16.915 --> 00:08:21.165
dot usgovcloudapi dot net, with
dot-us certificate-check names too.

117
00:08:21.165 --> 00:08:25.780
The agent uses outbound port
443 for service traffic and port

118
00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:30.577
80 for certificate revocation
lists, and needs no inbound ports.

119
00:08:30.577 --> 00:08:33.283
Per Microsoft Learn, from search excerpts:

120
00:08:33.283 --> 00:08:36.684
public DNS for these names
is a chain of CNAME records,

121
00:08:36.684 --> 00:08:41.939
you should avoid inline TLS inspection,
meaning a proxy that decrypts the traffic,

122
00:08:41.939 --> 00:08:47.040
and the agent installs to the default
Azure environment unless told otherwise.

123
00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.490
So keep a per-cloud list of names,
and test through the real egress path.

124
00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:56.898
Sources: Microsoft Learn: Microsoft
Graph national cloud deployments [S2];

125
00:08:56.898 --> 00:09:03.015
Prerequisites for Microsoft Entra Cloud
Sync, firewall and proxy requirements [S4];

126
00:09:03.015 --> 00:09:06.030
Cloud sync troubleshooting
[S4b (excerpt)];

127
00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:11.704
On-premises application provisioning
architecture [S6 (excerpt)]; Install the

128
00:09:11.704 --> 00:09:14.718
provisioning agent and Hybrid identity

129
00:09:14.718 --> 00:09:18.710
considerations for Azure
Government [S7 (excerpt)].

130
00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:21.710
Now Oracle, and a common mistake.

131
00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:25.636
Oracle Fusion offers private access
from on-premises, which Oracle

132
00:09:25.636 --> 00:09:30.122
describes as reaching your environment
without going through the internet.

133
00:09:30.122 --> 00:09:32.623
It sounds like a DNS feature.

134
00:09:32.623 --> 00:09:33.448
It isn't.

135
00:09:33.448 --> 00:09:34.347
It's routing.

136
00:09:34.347 --> 00:09:38.719
Traffic goes over FastConnect,
Oracle's private circuit,

137
00:09:38.719 --> 00:09:43.461
or a site-to-site VPN, into an
Oracle virtual cloud network, a VCN.

138
00:09:43.461 --> 00:09:46.621
From there it goes through
the VCN's Service Gateway

139
00:09:46.621 --> 00:09:49.635
to the Oracle Services
Network's public IP ranges.

140
00:09:49.635 --> 00:09:52.398
You add an access rule
for the VCN and turn

141
00:09:52.398 --> 00:09:55.323
off content acceleration,
Oracle's caching.

142
00:09:55.323 --> 00:09:59.285
Neither Oracle page says
anything about DNS.

143
00:09:59.285 --> 00:10:02.798
As an inference, the Fusion
name keeps its public answer.

144
00:10:02.798 --> 00:10:05.135
So don't build a private zone for Fusion.

145
00:10:05.135 --> 00:10:06.798
Second, Atom feeds.

146
00:10:06.798 --> 00:10:09.900
Oracle says Atom data can
be periodically purged,

147
00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:13.835
and Microsoft warns that delaying
Atom feeds can lose changes.

148
00:10:13.835 --> 00:10:17.695
As an inference, if the
middleware loses DNS or internet

149
00:10:17.695 --> 00:10:21.723
access for longer than the purge
window, HR events are lost.

150
00:10:21.723 --> 00:10:25.622
Middleware DNS is a correctness
issue, not just uptime.

151
00:10:25.622 --> 00:10:28.048
Reconcile against full extracts.

152
00:10:28.048 --> 00:10:29.560
Third, FedRAMP.

153
00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:33.685
The Marketplace lists Oracle
Fusion Cloud at FedRAMP Moderate.

154
00:10:33.685 --> 00:10:38.337
Oracle's government cloud regions
support private DNS zones,

155
00:10:38.337 --> 00:10:41.073
but public DNS isn't available there.

156
00:10:41.073 --> 00:10:45.609
Which Oracle environment hosts a
given agency's tenant wasn't verified.

157
00:10:45.609 --> 00:10:49.541
Sources: Oracle: OCI Fusion Applications,

158
00:10:49.541 --> 00:10:53.934
Securely Accessing Fusion
Applications [S21];

159
00:10:53.934 --> 00:10:59.598
Transit Routing for Private
Access to Oracle Services [S22];

160
00:10:59.598 --> 00:11:04.454
REST API for Fusion Cloud
HCM, Working with Atom [S18];

161
00:11:04.454 --> 00:11:11.159
US Government Cloud with FedRAMP
authorization [S26]; Microsoft Learn,

162
00:11:11.159 --> 00:11:14.743
Oracle HCM provisioning tutorial [S1];

163
00:11:14.743 --> 00:11:20.890
FedRAMP Marketplace products.json,
record F1603257882 [S40].

164
00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:27.478
Two more components: Amazon Connect,
AWS's contact center service, and VMware.

165
00:11:27.478 --> 00:11:31.618
Connect agents work in a browser,
in the Contact Control Panel,

166
00:11:31.618 --> 00:11:35.353
and AWS says that traffic
traverses the public network.

167
00:11:35.353 --> 00:11:41.369
AWS prefers a domain allowlist: the
instance name under my dot connect dot aws,

168
00:11:41.369 --> 00:11:47.103
plus telemetry, participant and transport
names, and media names for TURN,

169
00:11:47.103 --> 00:11:51.052
a relay that carries the call
audio, on UDP port 3478.

170
00:11:51.052 --> 00:11:54.096
If agents' DNS can't
resolve those TURN names,

171
00:11:54.096 --> 00:11:57.140
the softphone can't set
up its media channel.

172
00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:00.215
So don't over-restrict DNS for agents.

173
00:12:00.215 --> 00:12:05.265
In AWS GovCloud, Connect is
available only in the US-West region.

174
00:12:05.265 --> 00:12:08.466
It uses names under govcloud
dot connect dot aws,

175
00:12:08.466 --> 00:12:13.188
has no cross-partition integration
with commercial regions, and lacks

176
00:12:13.188 --> 00:12:18.390
several features, including Customer
Profiles, Cases and outbound campaigns.

177
00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:24.671
AWS PrivateLink, which gives an AWS API
a private address inside your network,

178
00:12:24.671 --> 00:12:27.615
covers only a subset of Connect APIs.

179
00:12:27.615 --> 00:12:33.465
As an inference, that helps workloads making
API calls, not the agent or voice path.

180
00:12:33.465 --> 00:12:37.170
A Lambda function attached
to a VPC can reach only

181
00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:40.965
that VPC unless you add
NAT or interface endpoints.

182
00:12:40.965 --> 00:12:46.395
VMware: a Broadcom knowledge article says
changing vCenter's hostname or address

183
00:12:46.395 --> 00:12:48.870
runs forward and reverse DNS lookups,

184
00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:52.703
and the change is blocked if
the name doesn't fully resolve.

185
00:12:52.703 --> 00:12:54.677
Keep those records right in the Grid.

186
00:12:54.677 --> 00:13:01.494
Sources: AWS: Amazon Connect, Set
up your network for the CCP [S27];

187
00:13:01.494 --> 00:13:06.840
AWS GovCloud (US) User Guide,
Connect Customer [S28];

188
00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:13.388
Amazon Connect and interface
VPC endpoints [S29]; AWS Lambda,

189
00:13:13.388 --> 00:13:18.867
Giving functions access to
resources in a VPC [S30];

190
00:13:18.867 --> 00:13:25.370
Broadcom KB 440783, VAMI hostname
or IP change fails [S36].

191
00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:27.395
Here's the subtle one.

192
00:13:27.395 --> 00:13:29.792
Suppose a cloud network has both a private

193
00:13:29.792 --> 00:13:32.257
zone and a forwarding
rule for the same name.

194
00:13:32.257 --> 00:13:33.296
Which wins?

195
00:13:33.296 --> 00:13:35.045
The three clouds disagree.

196
00:13:35.045 --> 00:13:40.870
In AWS, the Route 53 Resolver rule
wins over the private hosted zone.

197
00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:45.672
In Azure, a linked private DNS zone
wins, and, per Microsoft Learn,

198
00:13:45.672 --> 00:13:49.458
the forwarding-ruleset
rule is silently ignored.

199
00:13:49.458 --> 00:13:54.244
In Oracle Cloud, the attached private
views are checked before resolver rules.

200
00:13:54.244 --> 00:13:58.970
Once a view answers, evaluation stops,
even if the answer is negative.

201
00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:00.682
That word negative matters.

202
00:14:00.682 --> 00:14:02.720
A private zone can shadow a name.

203
00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:07.350
If the zone covers a name but has no
record for it, it returns NXDOMAIN,

204
00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:11.732
which means the name doesn't exist,
instead of asking anyone else.

205
00:14:11.732 --> 00:14:14.383
All three clouds document that behavior.

206
00:14:14.383 --> 00:14:17.019
So, as an inference, the same zone and the

207
00:14:17.019 --> 00:14:20.232
same rule give different
answers in each cloud.

208
00:14:20.232 --> 00:14:24.258
The only design that behaves the same
everywhere is one owner per name.

209
00:14:24.258 --> 00:14:26.421
The Grid owns agency zones.

210
00:14:26.421 --> 00:14:28.845
Each cloud owns its own private zones.

211
00:14:28.845 --> 00:14:30.957
Everything else forwards to the owner.

212
00:14:30.957 --> 00:14:34.579
Never put a zone and a rule for
the same name in one network,

213
00:14:34.579 --> 00:14:37.044
and test resolution from every origin,

214
00:14:37.044 --> 00:14:41.358
because a configuration that works
in one cloud can fail in the next.

215
00:14:41.358 --> 00:14:48.743
Sources: AWS, Route 53, Considerations
when working with a private hosted zone

216
00:14:48.743 --> 00:14:56.244
[S25]; Microsoft Learn, Troubleshoot
NXDOMAIN from an Azure Private DNS zone [S12

217
00:14:56.244 --> 00:14:59.591
(excerpt)]; Oracle, OCI Private DNS [S23];

218
00:14:59.591 --> 00:15:04.580
research brief, Bottom line
4 and failure mode F3.

219
00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:06.455
How does this break?

220
00:15:06.455 --> 00:15:09.805
Here are seven failures from
the brief's table of sixteen.

221
00:15:09.805 --> 00:15:11.830
The full table is in the backup.

222
00:15:11.830 --> 00:15:13.030
One, shadowing.

223
00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:16.490
Someone creates a private
zone for a vendor's domain,

224
00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:21.480
say oraclecloud dot com, and every name
not copied into it returns NXDOMAIN.

225
00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:23.192
Two, silent fallback.

226
00:15:23.192 --> 00:15:26.671
A private endpoint name
resolves to the public address,

227
00:15:26.671 --> 00:15:29.468
because a forwarding
rule is missing or slow.

228
00:15:29.468 --> 00:15:31.363
Per a Microsoft Learn excerpt,

229
00:15:31.363 --> 00:15:35.608
Windows DNS servers that forward
to Azure need a forwarder timeout

230
00:15:35.608 --> 00:15:40.081
above four seconds, or private records
may resolve to public addresses.

231
00:15:40.081 --> 00:15:42.292
Three, the Grid's own fallback.

232
00:15:42.292 --> 00:15:46.926
Infoblox documents that if Use Forwarders
Only is off and the forwarders fail,

233
00:15:46.926 --> 00:15:50.918
the member falls back to resolving
from the internet's root servers.

234
00:15:50.918 --> 00:15:53.270
As an inference, a failed forward can then

235
00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:55.830
return the public answer
instead of failing.

236
00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:56.905
Four, loops.

237
00:15:56.905 --> 00:16:00.647
Azure warns that a ruleset rule
that targets an inbound endpoint

238
00:16:00.647 --> 00:16:04.043
must not be linked to the network
where that endpoint lives.

239
00:16:04.043 --> 00:16:06.556
Five, TLS name mismatch.

240
00:16:06.556 --> 00:16:09.414
Keep the public name in
every connection string,

241
00:16:09.414 --> 00:16:12.130
because the service's
certificate carries it.

242
00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:16.968
Six, the wrong cloud's endpoints, as
we saw on the government-cloud slide.

243
00:16:16.968 --> 00:16:18.543
Seven, capacity.

244
00:16:18.543 --> 00:16:23.575
A Route 53 Resolver endpoint address handles
about ten thousand queries per second,

245
00:16:23.575 --> 00:16:26.881
but as few as fifteen hundred
with connection tracking.

246
00:16:26.881 --> 00:16:29.918
Size endpoints before
each onboarding wave.

247
00:16:29.918 --> 00:16:38.496
Sources: Research brief §6, failure
modes F1, F4, F5, F6, F9, F10, F11:

248
00:16:38.496 --> 00:16:48.650
Microsoft Learn [S2, S10 (excerpt), S10b
(excerpt), S12 (excerpt), S14b (excerpt)];

249
00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:58.278
AWS [S25, S32]; Oracle [S23]; Infoblox
NIOS 9.1, Specifying Forwarders [S37];

250
00:16:58.278 --> 00:17:07.210
the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08 Ch02 and Ch10.

251
00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:10.860
With several clouds, how do
you wire the forwarding?

252
00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:12.697
Every vendor documents a hub.

253
00:17:12.697 --> 00:17:14.272
None recommends a mesh.

254
00:17:14.272 --> 00:17:19.817
Azure documents two hub designs: link one
forwarding ruleset to every spoke network,

255
00:17:19.817 --> 00:17:22.985
or point the spokes at the
hub's inbound endpoint.

256
00:17:22.985 --> 00:17:28.135
AWS shares Resolver rules from one
account through Resource Access Manager.

257
00:17:28.135 --> 00:17:31.460
Oracle shares a view across
networks in a region.

258
00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:33.598
Here's some illustrative arithmetic.

259
00:17:33.598 --> 00:17:40.335
Take five environments: on-premises,
Azure, AWS, Oracle Cloud and VMware.

260
00:17:40.335 --> 00:17:44.097
In a full mesh, each forwards
directly to every other,

261
00:17:44.097 --> 00:17:48.122
so you need up to twenty one-way
rule sets, five times four.

262
00:17:48.122 --> 00:17:52.622
With a hub, you need about five, one
per environment pointing at the hub.

263
00:17:52.622 --> 00:17:57.057
As an inference, every extra path is
one more place for the precedence,

264
00:17:57.057 --> 00:17:59.547
fallback and loop failures we just saw.

265
00:17:59.547 --> 00:18:01.198
Where does Infoblox fit?

266
00:18:01.198 --> 00:18:04.473
The Grid is a natural
hub for agency names.

267
00:18:04.473 --> 00:18:08.496
Each Grid member can have its own
forwarders for a forward zone,

268
00:18:08.496 --> 00:18:12.752
and Infoblox's reference design
puts vNIOS, its virtual appliance,

269
00:18:12.752 --> 00:18:15.847
in a shared-services hub
network as a Grid member.

270
00:18:15.847 --> 00:18:19.662
The earlier multi-cloud research,
labeled as design guidance,

271
00:18:19.662 --> 00:18:23.851
recommends a baseline of the Grid
plus each cloud's native resolver,

272
00:18:23.851 --> 00:18:29.161
forwarding both ways, with customer-operated
appliances for FedRAMP High workloads,

273
00:18:29.161 --> 00:18:33.873
because Infoblox's software-as-a-service
offering is FedRAMP Moderate only.

274
00:18:33.873 --> 00:18:42.029
Sources: Microsoft Learn, Azure DNS Private
Resolver endpoints and rulesets [S10

275
00:18:42.029 --> 00:18:49.090
(excerpt)] and Private resolver architecture
[S10c (excerpt)]; AWS, Route 53,

276
00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:56.637
Managing forwarding rules [S33]; Oracle,
OCI Private DNS [S23]; Infoblox NIOS 9.1,

277
00:18:56.637 --> 00:19:02.237
Configuring a Forward Zone
[S38]; research_multicloud.md,

278
00:19:02.237 --> 00:19:09.419
Bottom line and Option A (Infoblox
Shared-Services VPC Deployments [S27]);

279
00:19:09.419 --> 00:19:14.280
mesh-versus-hub arithmetic
is illustrative.

280
00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:16.680
So what must the agency do?

281
00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:20.186
This is program design guidance,
not a vendor requirement,

282
00:19:20.186 --> 00:19:22.742
condensed from a thirteen-step checklist.

283
00:19:22.742 --> 00:19:24.505
Names and owners first.

284
00:19:24.505 --> 00:19:28.393
Inventory every name in
the HR path, per cloud.

285
00:19:28.393 --> 00:19:31.317
Never create a private zone
for a vendor's domain.

286
00:19:31.317 --> 00:19:35.817
Give each agency namespace one
owner, and forward everything else.

287
00:19:35.817 --> 00:19:40.655
And never let a zone and a rule for
the same name exist in one network.

288
00:19:40.655 --> 00:19:41.493
Forwarding.

289
00:19:41.493 --> 00:19:46.807
From the Grid, forward each Azure service's
public zone, not its privatelink zone,

290
00:19:46.807 --> 00:19:52.042
to inbound endpoints in two regions, and
set Use Forwarders Only on those zones.

291
00:19:52.042 --> 00:19:55.767
Raise Windows forwarder
timeouts above four seconds.

292
00:19:55.767 --> 00:20:01.505
Run a hub in each cloud, and review the
forwarding graph for loops at every change.

293
00:20:01.505 --> 00:20:06.593
Size resolver endpoints before
each HR 2.0 onboarding wave.

294
00:20:06.593 --> 00:20:07.630
Components.

295
00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:12.705
Put the middleware in a Logic Apps Standard
or Functions plan joined to a virtual

296
00:20:12.705 --> 00:20:15.205
network, using the agency resolver path,

297
00:20:15.205 --> 00:20:18.917
and keep Lifecycle Workflow
extensions to public targets.

298
00:20:18.917 --> 00:20:23.278
Run at least two provisioning agents
dedicated to HR provisioning,

299
00:20:23.278 --> 00:20:27.093
near preferred domain controllers,
with no TLS inspection.

300
00:20:27.093 --> 00:20:30.644
Treat middleware DNS as
a data-integrity control,

301
00:20:30.644 --> 00:20:33.329
and reconcile against full extracts.

302
00:20:33.329 --> 00:20:36.005
For Connect, let the TURN names resolve.

303
00:20:36.005 --> 00:20:39.693
For VMware, keep A and
PTR records in the Grid.

304
00:20:39.693 --> 00:20:41.606
And test from every origin.

305
00:20:41.606 --> 00:20:45.818
Any public answer or NXDOMAIN
for a private name is a defect.

306
00:20:45.818 --> 00:20:50.099
Sources: Research brief §9,

307
00:20:50.099 --> 00:21:03.555
Agency checklist steps 1–13 (all PROGRAM
DESIGN GUIDANCE) and the facts each step

308
00:21:03.555 --> 00:21:13.953
cites: Microsoft Learn [S1, S2, S4, S4b,
S7b, S8, S10, S10c, S11b, S12, S12b,

309
00:21:13.953 --> 00:21:24.962
S13–S15, S14b]; Oracle [S18, S21–S23]; AWS
[S25, S27–S30, S32, S33]; Broadcom [S36];

310
00:21:24.962 --> 00:21:35.767
Infoblox [S37]; the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 03 Ch03,

311
00:21:35.767 --> 00:21:41.420
Ch05; Book 08 Ch02, Ch07, Ch08, Ch10.

312
00:21:42.170 --> 00:21:43.307
The decisions.

313
00:21:43.307 --> 00:21:46.758
Who owns each namespace,
the Grid or a cloud.

314
00:21:46.758 --> 00:21:51.058
Which middleware platform, and so which
network and resolver path it uses.

315
00:21:51.058 --> 00:21:53.020
The hub design in each cloud.

316
00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:56.434
And whether Oracle's private
access is needed at all,

317
00:21:56.434 --> 00:21:58.895
remembering that it's routing, not DNS.

318
00:21:58.895 --> 00:22:01.370
The next steps are the
things I couldn't verify.

319
00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:07.195
Which Oracle environment and hostnames
the agency's Core HCM tenant will use.

320
00:22:07.195 --> 00:22:11.206
Whether API-driven inbound
provisioning is available in the U.S.

321
00:22:11.206 --> 00:22:12.720
Government Graph cloud.

322
00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:16.861
Whether Azure's DNS Private
Resolver, Lifecycle Workflows and

323
00:22:16.861 --> 00:22:21.320
Connect private endpoints are
available in the government clouds.

324
00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:24.687
And the VMware install
guides' DNS rules and Oracle's

325
00:22:24.687 --> 00:22:27.820
own connectivity patterns,
which I couldn't open.

326
00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:29.495
They're all listed in the backup.

327
00:22:29.495 --> 00:22:30.632
Where does this fit?

328
00:22:30.632 --> 00:22:36.413
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 03 covers HR-driven identity,

329
00:22:36.413 --> 00:22:42.483
with Federal HR 2.0 in Chapter 03 and
the provisioning pipeline in Chapter 05.

330
00:22:42.483 --> 00:22:49.119
Book 08 covers DNS, DHCP and IP address
management: split-brain DNS in Chapter 02,

331
00:22:49.119 --> 00:22:55.960
cloud private endpoints in Chapter 07,
the reference architecture in Chapter 08,

332
00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:59.432
and the migration sequence in Chapter 10.

333
00:22:59.432 --> 00:23:05.771
Three research items sit beside this one:
Trinzic Alone, or Trinzic Plus Cloud DDI;

334
00:23:05.771 --> 00:23:11.069
Infoblox DDI for Federal Agencies;
and Who Names the Device After AD.

335
00:23:11.069 --> 00:23:14.565
One note on standing: this
is research under review,

336
00:23:14.565 --> 00:23:17.395
not an endorsement by any federal agency.

337
